  "query_preview": "..\nTonight and Thursday: A trough will be deepening off the coast \nand bring a dry southerly flow pattern through Thursday. It will\npromote a fairly benign day with highs in the upper 70s and \n80s. Winds will be light. Only chance of precip will be in the \nfar North Cascades with little to accumulation expected. Tonight\nlows will be in the 40s and low 50s for most. Higher elevations\ncould dip into the mid 30s for the higher terrain of the \nCascades and northern mountains.\n\nThursday Night and Friday: The trough will move East and push a\nstrong Low across the Inland Northwest. The Low will bring \n0.80-1 inch of precipitable water with it. Guidance has been \nconsistent with the track and has been slightly increasing the \nprecip amounts and wind speeds. Starting mid morning, the Low \nwill swing a cold front through the region starting Friday \nmorning and into the early afternoon. It will bring widespread \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms. It will be followed by gusty\nwinds across the Basin. Highs will be drastically lower on \nFriday in the upper 50s and 60s.\n\nFor precip amounts, guidance has +50% probability of at least a\ntenth of an inch across the region. Areas that will see the \nhighest rainfall include the Cascades, northern mountains, Idaho\nPanhandle. Rain amounts between 0.25-0.50 of an inch are \nlikely. Localized total between 0.50-1.00 inch are possible near\nthe Cascade crest and northern mountains. Across the Basin, \namounts will be in the 0.20-0.40 of inch. Snow levels along the \nCascades drop to around 6000 ft with the frontal passage and \nbring an increased chance of snow Friday morning for the \nCascades. While amounts are not expected to be more than a few \ninches, a convective shower could bring a quick burst of heavy \nsnow. Hikers should plan accordingly for possible winter \nweather. \n\nFor thunderstorms, ensembles bring the best chances late \nmorning to middle afternoon. Moderate Theta-e values across the \nregion support convection with the front passage. As the front \npasses, indices Cape values are in the 200-400 J/kg. Lapse rates\nare +6. The best chances are over the Cascades and northern \nmountains. These areas have 20-30 percent chance for \nthunderstorms. The region has less than 20 percent chance. Main \nimpacts will be lightning, gusty outflows, and brief heavy \ndownpours. \n\nCold, dry air will fill in behind the exiting front. Winds in \nthe 850-700mb range from 35-45 mph. Models indicate a sharp drop\nin the Theta-e values behind the cold front. It will allow the \nlow level winds to mix to the surface and bring gusty winds to \nmost of the Basin into the early evening. Agreement between the \nmodels on where highest winds will occur is not great. The NBM \nputs the strongest over the Waterville Plateau. The other \nensembles put the winds in extreme eastern Washington. Across \nthe basin, there is at least a 40 percent probability for gusts \n35 mph or greater across the Basin. There is 50 percent \nprobability for winds 40+ mph depending on if the which guidance\nis correct. An isolated pocket of 50+ mph is possible but a \nless than 5 percent probability. Blowing dust is possible but is\ndependent on the amount of rain from earlier in the day. \nPossible impacts include airborne objects, difficult travel for \nhigh profile vehicles, and isolated power outages.\n\nSaturday through Tuesday: Behind the system, the region will be\nunder the influence of a cool, dry shallow trough pattern. It \nwill definitely feel like autumn across the region. Highs will \nbe in the upper 50s to low 60s. Overnight lows will be in the \nmid 30s to 40s. Frost could be an issue for areas in the \nCascades and northern mountains. /JDC",
